1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:03,265
Imagine a machine that prints brand-new
money.

2
00:00:03,315 --> 00:00:05,557
If a million new dollars came out
tomorrow,

3
00:00:05,607 --> 00:00:07,453
would it matter who got them first?

4
00:00:07,903 --> 00:00:12,311
It does. It may be why your rent keeps
climbing, and your savings keep shrinking.

5
00:00:12,361 --> 00:00:14,282
It's called the Cantillon Effect.

6
00:00:15,232 --> 00:00:20,051
Three hundred years ago, a Paris banker
named Richard Cantillon noticed something.

7
00:00:20,151 --> 00:00:22,088
New money doesn't spread evenly.

8
00:00:22,138 --> 00:00:24,847
It enters at one point, and ripples
outward.

9
00:00:25,597 --> 00:00:28,587
Picture a tiny town where bread costs one
dollar.

10
00:00:28,687 --> 00:00:30,643
New money goes to the banker first.

11
00:00:30,693 --> 00:00:33,722
He spends it right away, at yesterday's
prices.

12
00:00:33,822 --> 00:00:37,338
Next, it reaches the builder. Prices start
to creep up.

13
00:00:37,388 --> 00:00:40,117
Then the shopkeeper. Bread hits a dollar
fifty.

14
00:00:40,217 --> 00:00:45,150
By the time it reaches the teacher and the
retiree, their incomes haven't changed.

15
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:47,109
But bread now costs two dollars.

16
00:00:47,309 --> 00:00:52,481
Nobody stole anything. But wealth quietly
moved, from the back of the line to the
front.

17
00:00:53,231 --> 00:00:57,387
Today, new money flows first to banks,
governments, and big investors,

18
00:00:57,437 --> 00:01:01,421
who buy assets like stocks and real estate
before prices move.

19
00:01:01,571 --> 00:01:03,831
Wages are almost always last.

20
00:01:04,581 --> 00:01:09,051
So if you earn a paycheck, your raise
shows up after prices have already moved.

21
00:01:09,201 --> 00:01:13,412
If you save in cash, it's like walking up
an escalator that's going down.

22
00:01:13,562 --> 00:01:18,535
Meanwhile, people who already own assets
get richer, without doing anything
differently.

23
00:01:19,285 --> 00:01:21,696
Inflation isn't just prices going up.

24
00:01:21,746 --> 00:01:22,775
It's a transfer.

25
00:01:22,975 --> 00:01:27,497
So next time you hear that new money is
being created, don't just ask how much.

26
00:01:27,547 --> 00:01:29,149
Ask: who gets it first?
